1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,348
On November 14, 2026, Mars is
1.324 astronomical units away.

2
00:00:05,388 --> 00:00:10,270
A question to the flight surgeon
takes eleven minutes to arrive

3
00:00:10,310 --> 00:00:14,882
and eleven more to come back.
There's no live consultation.

4
00:00:14,922 --> 00:00:19,960
Whatever the crew needs to know,
they have to know on board.

5
00:00:20,000 --> 00:00:24,064
EMMA gathers readings from a
wearable, a vigilance test,

6
00:00:24,104 --> 00:00:27,902
a diary, an ultrasound
or a scanned report.

7
00:00:27,942 --> 00:00:32,340
Each reading goes into a standard
clinical format and gets signed.

8
00:00:32,380 --> 00:00:35,712
It's compared with that
person's own baseline,

9
00:00:35,752 --> 00:00:38,017
not just with a population range.

10
00:00:38,057 --> 00:00:42,721
Every part of the body can be tapped:
what measures it, how it's doing

11
00:00:42,761 --> 00:00:44,960
and what evidence lies behind it.

12
00:00:45,000 --> 00:00:47,515
The console is a tablet with tabs.

13
00:00:47,555 --> 00:00:52,004
First come the five spaceflight
hazards NASA defines.

14
00:00:52,044 --> 00:00:54,171
The setting changes what matters:

15
00:00:54,211 --> 00:00:57,435
low orbit is not the Moon or Mars.

16
00:00:57,475 --> 00:01:00,054
In microgravity there is no up or down.

17
00:01:00,094 --> 00:01:04,414
Following the gyroscope, or
the viewer's face as a demo,

18
00:01:04,454 --> 00:01:06,582
an arrow points to the real up.

19
00:01:06,622 --> 00:01:10,104
There's large text, high
contrast and a privacy mode.

20
00:01:10,144 --> 00:01:13,174
The console works with a screen reader.

21
00:01:13,214 --> 00:01:17,212
And if someone can't look, Escape
three times or three fingers

22
00:01:17,252 --> 00:01:19,960
turn on the voice and read out the status.

23
00:01:20,000 --> 00:01:25,969
And you can talk to it. Just say
"EMMA", without touching the screen:

24
00:01:26,009 --> 00:01:27,999
"EMMA, how am I doing?"

25
00:01:28,039 --> 00:01:34,441
The wake word, the transcription and
the language model run on the device,

26
00:01:34,481 --> 00:01:39,931
offline. Whatever the model
writes is labelled as unvalidated.

27
00:01:39,971 --> 00:01:44,297
It can log a diary entry
or acknowledge an alert,

28
00:01:44,337 --> 00:01:49,960
but it signs nothing until the
person says "confirmo", I confirm.

29
00:01:50,000 --> 00:01:54,675
A solar particle event arrives,
and the spacecraft is offline.

30
00:01:54,715 --> 00:01:59,692
The onboard dosimeter is enough:
while the dose is over its limit,

31
00:01:59,732 --> 00:02:04,407
EMMA shortens the measurements
that matter most, and says why.

32
00:02:04,447 --> 00:02:10,329
When something changes, it explains what
changed, since when and against what,

33
00:02:10,369 --> 00:02:14,365
what is worth checking and
what evidence supports it.

34
00:02:14,405 --> 00:02:19,985
It recommends; it doesn't diagnose
or prescribe. The crew decides.

35
00:02:20,025 --> 00:02:24,700
The demo also shows the way back:
the dose falls, the routines

36
00:02:24,740 --> 00:02:29,491
return to their pace and,
days later, no alert remains.

37
00:02:29,531 --> 00:02:34,960
On the ground they see it later, with
the delay, and the screen says so.

38
00:02:35,000 --> 00:02:41,103
On a spacecraft everything is
scarce: power, time, hands.

39
00:02:41,143 --> 00:02:48,102
The crew can declare a reduced
or minimal mode, signed,

40
00:02:48,142 --> 00:02:54,031
and EMMA stops asking for
what is costliest to measure.

41
00:02:54,071 --> 00:02:59,960
What never switches off is
the analysis and the alerts.

42
00:03:00,000 --> 00:03:05,510
What leaves the spacecraft?
One view says it, item by item:

43
00:03:05,550 --> 00:03:12,647
what the ground sees, what stays on
board and what never leaves the console.

44
00:03:12,687 --> 00:03:17,729
And where the code doesn't
enforce it, it says so too.

45
00:03:17,769 --> 00:03:24,960
Every data point goes into a record that
can't be altered without it showing.

46
00:03:25,000 --> 00:03:28,756
We tested it on real human physiology:

47
00:03:28,796 --> 00:03:33,708
of 22 healthy people,
21 get no alert at all.

48
00:03:33,748 --> 00:03:38,852
On that same physiology,
with injected deterioration,

49
00:03:38,892 --> 00:03:43,419
it detects 95% of sustained tachycardias.

50
00:03:43,459 --> 00:03:50,490
It misses slow drifts, and with
weeks of data it alerts too often.

51
00:03:50,530 --> 00:03:54,960
That's on Earth, not in
flight, and we say so.

52
00:03:55,000 --> 00:04:00,831
In 1970, Ernst Stuhlinger
answered a nun who asked

53
00:04:00,871 --> 00:04:06,514
why spend on going to Mars while
children were going hungry:

54
00:04:06,554 --> 00:04:11,351
the extreme challenges
of space force solutions

55
00:04:11,391 --> 00:04:14,401
that later come back to Earth.

56
00:04:14,441 --> 00:04:21,495
What works 22 minutes from Earth
works wherever help can't reach:

57
00:04:21,535 --> 00:04:27,178
a mountain hut, a village
cut off by a flood.

58
00:04:27,218 --> 00:04:34,178
It isn't the same medicine: it's the
same architecture, offline and local.

59
00:04:34,218 --> 00:04:41,272
Taking it there, and into schools, is
product vision: it isn't built today.

60
00:04:41,312 --> 00:04:45,920
EMMA works without internet
and it's open source.

61
00:04:45,960 --> 00:04:51,697
Developed in accordance with
the IEC 62304 standard, Class B;

62
00:04:51,737 --> 00:04:54,841
it claims no certification.

63
00:04:54,881 --> 00:04:59,960
EMMA: decision support
when Earth is far away.
